'''Tramuntana''' ({{lang|ca|Tramuntana}}, from Catalan ''tramuntana'' "north wind") is the northernmost [[Provinces of Balboa|province]] of [[Balboa]], occupying the northern portion of the [[Balboa isthmus]] along the [[Shendan Ocean]] coast. It is bordered by [[Jala Province]] to the south, with the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range]] dividing the two, by [[Nurra Province]] to the east, across the Yalabirga watershed divide, and by [[Tapuya]] to the north. The province has an area of 109,965 km² and a population of approximately 1.9 million (2026 estimate), making it the most sparsely populated province in Balboa. The provincial capital is [[Portblanc]], a port city on the Shendan coast.

Tramuntana is primarily an agricultural and resource province. The highland valleys support subsistence agriculture — sorghum, millet, and cassava — and livestock grazing on cooperative farms, while the Shendan coastal waters are the site of Balboa's offshore oil extraction operations and a significant aquaculture industry. The province's remote northern highlands contain communities of the [[Birú people]], the indigenous inhabitants of the isthmus, who have maintained a presence in the region throughout the colonial and post-colonial periods. The [[Portblanc Military Academy]], the principal military training institution in Balboa, is located in the provincial capital. Tramuntana is the poorest province in Balboa, with a GDP per capita of approximately $2,400 and a poverty rate of 42.6 per cent — nearly five times the national average.

The province is the least densely populated in Balboa, with an overall density of approximately 17 inhabitants per km². The population is heavily concentrated in Portblanc and its surrounding comarca, which together account for over a third of the provincial population. The remaining comarcas are predominantly rural, with economies based on agriculture, fishing, and small-scale forestry.

