[[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] are a smaller presence in Tramuntana than in the central and southern provinces, though communities exist in Portblanc and the coastal towns, descended from enslaved workers brought to the colonial plantations and from internal migration in the post-reunification period.

== Economy ==

Tramuntana is the poorest province in Balboa, with a GDP per capita of approximately $2,400 — less than one-sixth that of [[Estret Province|Estret]] — and a poverty rate of 42.6 per cent, the highest in the country. The province's economy is based on subsistence agriculture in the highland valleys, cattle ranching on the savanna, and fishing and aquaculture along the coast.

Cattle ranching is the primary agricultural activity. The Tramuntana savanna, with its drier rain-shadow climate, is better suited to grazing than to crop production. [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]], the state-owned agricultural corporation, operates cattle ranches in the province, and beef is produced primarily for domestic consumption. Cotton is also grown on the savanna, though at a smaller scale, and is sold to the domestic textile industry.

Subsistence agriculture in the highland valleys employs an estimated 80,000–100,000 people, who grow maize, sorghum, millet, and cassava outside the formal economy. These communities are disproportionately [[Birú people|Birú]] and poor white Balboan, and they have the lowest incomes and highest poverty rates in the country. Food security is a concern in the highland valleys, where subsistence farming communities have limited access to markets and the overvalued [[International Dollar]] makes purchased food expensive relative to local incomes.

Aquaculture is a growing sector along the Shendan coast. Shrimp farming, in particular, has developed in the mangrove nurseries of the coastal zone, and certain shrimp species endemic to Balboa's coastal waters are inherently competitive on the international market regardless of the overvalued ID. Marine fisheries also operate from Portblanc and the smaller coastal towns.

[[File:Tramuntana shrimp farm Shendan coast.png|thumb|right|300px|A shrimp farm on the Shendan coast near Portblanc.]]
<!-- IMAGE PROMPT: Coastal shrimp aquaculture farm on a tropical shoreline, rectangular ponds divided by earthen dykes, nets and aerators visible, mangrove forest in the background, small wooden processing shed, workers in waders tending ponds, overcast tropical sky, photorealistic, aerial view, muted green and brown tones -->

The [[Corporació Petrolífera de l'Estat de Balboa]] (CPEB), the state-owned petroleum company, manages offshore oil extraction in the deepwater Shendan Sea off the Tramuntana coast, near the outer limit of Balboa's exclusive economic zone. The offshore fields produce approximately 185,000 barrels per day, mostly for domestic consumption. Crude is processed at [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery in neighbouring Jala Province under supply agreements.

Tourism is a small but growing sector, centred on the cloud forest eco-tourism of the Yalabirga highlands, though the sector is limited by infrastructure and the province's remoteness. The provincial capital, Portblanc, is the principal commercial and administrative centre. The city's economy is dominated by port activities, government services, and the [[Portblanc Military Academy]], which is one of the largest employers in the province.

== Transport ==

{{see also|Transport in Balboa}}

[[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]], the principal north–south artery, runs through Tramuntana from the Jala border in the south to the far northern comarcas, passing through Portblanc. [[Expressway 2 (Balboa)|Expressway 2]] runs east–west through the central comarcas, connecting the inland eastern comarcas to Portblanc on the Shendan coast. [[Expressway 8 (Balboa)|Expressway 8]] runs east–west through the northern comarcas, connecting the Nurra border region to the Shendan coast via Portblanc. [[Expressway 116 (Balboa)|Expressway 116]] is a short connector between Portblanc and the central transport hub of [[La Cruïlla (comarca)|La Cruïlla]].

The [[Portblanc Line]], operated by [[Ferrocarrils de l'Istme]] (FI), is the principal railway in the province, running from [[Sant Bart]] in Jala Province through the southern comarcas to Portblanc. The line is primarily a freight corridor carrying agricultural products and petroleum, but operates a limited passenger service. A streetcar system operates in Portblanc.

The province is served by [[Portblanc Airport]], which offers domestic flights to Sant Cristòfor, Sant Bart, and other destinations.

== History ==

{{main|History of Balboa}}

=== Pre-colonial and colonial period ===

Prior to Volisanian colonisation, the Tramuntana highlands were inhabited by the [[Birú people]], who maintained settlements in the highland valleys and footpaths across the Yalabirga. The Birú highland confederation controlled the northern portage corridors connecting the Shendan coast to the interior, and Birú goldwork was traded across the isthmus.

The Volisanian colonial administration established cattle ranches on the Tramuntana savanna, where the dry rain-shadow climate was better suited to grazing than to the plantation crops of the Aethiopian coast. Cotton was also cultivated on a smaller scale. Portblanc was developed as a Shendan coast port, serving as a staging point for maritime trade and as a naval station. The Birú population was devastated by conquest, disease, and forced labour, and the survivors retreated into the remote northern highlands.

=== Rump state period ===

During the rump state period (1899–1973), Tramuntana was the northern frontier of the truncated republic. Portblanc, on the Shendan coast, served as the principal western port of the rump state, handling maritime trade that could not pass through the Zong-controlled canal. The [[Portblanc Military Academy]] was established during this period to train officers for the rump-state armed forces. The southern comarcas along the rail corridor to Sant Bart developed as agricultural suppliers to the rump-state capital, while the northern comarcas remained remote and largely outside the effective reach of the state.

[[File:Portblanc rump state era.png|thumb|right|300px|Portblanc during the rump state period, with the military academy visible on the headland.]]
<!-- IMAGE PROMPT: Small tropical port city in the mid-20th century, low-rise colonial buildings along a waterfront, fishing boats and cargo vessels in a modest harbour, military academy complex on a rocky headland overlooking the bay, dry scrub-covered hills behind the town, black-and-white historical photograph, 1950s era, documentary style, slightly grainy -->

=== Post-reunification era ===

The reunification of 1973 reintegrated the Sant Cristòfor and canal territories into the Balboan state. Tramuntana lost its strategic importance as the rump state's northern frontier, but the province benefited from national infrastructure investment, including the extension of the expressway network and the development of the offshore oil fields in the 1980s. The cooperative farm system was established in the highland valleys, replacing the colonial-era cattle ranches, though the dry climate limited the productivity of cooperative agriculture. The post-reunification period also saw the expansion of Portblanc as a commercial and administrative centre and the development of aquaculture operations along the Shendan coast.

The [[Tramuntana Regionalist Party]] (PRT), a regionalist party advocating greater provincial autonomy, has been a minor but persistent presence in Tramuntana politics, reflecting the province's peripheral status within the Balboan state.

